{
  "version": 1,
  "note": "What a client owes the server when the connection comes back. A gap is not just a pause: beliefs formed before it may be stale (a peer who stopped typing while the socket was down never sent the stop), server-side watches die with the stream that carried them (presence), and changes that happened during the gap were never delivered. Until R8 none of the five apps did any of the three: they subscribed and read when the conversation list loaded and when a chat opened, and a reconnect is neither. Every phase other than online, idle and actionRequired is a gap once the client has been online: connecting, syncing, switching (a new line is a new connection), reconnecting, waitingForNetwork and serviceUnavailable. The cases that must NOT refresh are sessions that ended — actionRequired (kicked, expired, an update required, the tenant suspended) and idle after a session (signed out): the core does not reconnect from them, and re-subscribing on the way out is work for a session that no longer exists.",
  "work": {
    "dropStaleBeliefs": "Forget what could have gone stale unobserved — the typing roster above all. A belief's own ttl would clear it eventually; the gap is the moment we know it is unreliable.",
    "resubscribe": "Ask the server again for what it was watching for us. A presence subscription is a stream, and a stream dies with its connection; the client core already forgets its own bookkeeping when the stream ends, so nothing but the app is stopping a fresh subscribe.",
    "reread": "Read the current value of what is on screen. A subscription tells you about changes from now on; it says nothing about the change you missed while you were away."
  },
  "driver": "Feed the phases in order; each one answers the work it creates. An unchanged phase creates none. Phases are the kit's `FlareConnectionPhase` values (the core SDK's `connection://status` phase).",
  "cases": [
